  "text": "Question two. What does a semantic layer change for an AI assistant? It moves the definitions out of the guess and into code. Metrics, their filters, the dimensions they can be sliced by, and the join paths between entities are declared once. The assistant's job shrinks from writing sequel to choosing: a metric, dimensions, filters and a time grain. The layer compiles the sequel. Ask for net revenue, and the assistant, the dashboards and the finance report all get four hundred forty, computed the same way. And a question the layer doesn't model returns an error saying no metric matches, not an invented query. A refusal is far cheaper than a wrong number in a board deck. So if an executive asks for active users last week, and nobody has defined active, the right answer says so, and offers the closest governed metrics, or an exploratory query labeled as unverified. Having the language model define it on the spot is metric drift by design: its version won't match the dashboards or finance. The price is coverage: the assistant can only answer what's been modeled. So many teams route modeled questions through the layer, and fall back to text-to-sequel, clearly labeled, for exploratory ones.",
